      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-03T18:23:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Again, if IOMMU driver init failed, we fail to enable DMA protection, so we should fail pKVM initialization?\n\nSo the approach from v1 (call pkvm_iommu_driver_init() explicitly in 2 places: as a part of pKVM initialization, and if pKVM initialization failed) seems more correct to me.",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d373a53d_6d02d350",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-03T20:08:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think failing DMA protection will leave the system in a unsecure state whether or not we fail pkvm initialization. In both cases(DMA failed, pkvm inited and DMA failed, pkvm uninited), we end up in a case where devices will be able to access the memory directly. The only difference being, system will be baremetal or virtualized. I feel this is a cleaner and readable code. I can put a banner saying system is not secure if DMA fails. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "0540fe90_839dbffa",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e3e3b45d_52528b9a",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-03T22:26:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we should be precise in what we mean by \"secure\", i.e. which security model we are talking about. Security within the host (i.e. whether the host kernel is able to protect itself from devices) is not pKVM\u0027s concern. The concern is whether pKVM is able to protect itself and pVMs from devices and from the host that controls those devices.\n\nIf your point is that if we failed to initialize IOMMU once, with pKVM, we don\u0027t need to try it again without pKVM, then I\u0027m ok with it.\n\nMy point is that if we failed to initialize IOMMU, we should\u0027t consider pKVM deprivilege succeeded, i.e. vmx_pkvm_init() should return failure and clear pvmfw [*] (and probably also reprivilege CPUs).\n\nIOW, if pKVM was implemented in such a way that it didn\u0027t need help from the host IOMMU driver to enable DMA protection, and the role of the host IOMMU driver was only to let the host itself use IOMMU, it would be a different story. But that\u0027s not the case, pKVM actually relies on intel_iommu_init() to make the PKVM_HC_IOMMU_SET_RTA hypercall to enable DMA protection, right?\n\n[*] BTW clearing pvmfw is crucial, I think. The bootloader passes pvmfw secrets to the kernel and trusts that the kernel will never have access to those secrets (either via CPU or DMA), except an early time window when the kernel has not exposed itself to all kinds of attack vectors yet. So if we don\u0027t enable DMA protection and yet don\u0027t clear pvmfw secrets, leaving them accessible via DMA by the host, we break this fundamental assumption.",
      "parentUuid": "d373a53d_6d02d350",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0ba32923_71fb8846",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-04T14:05:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ahh ok understood. What I meant was - when the user specifies \"kvm-intel.pkvm\u003d1\", its pkvm responsibility to enforce a secure state and if there is a failure in between, we can\u0027t bail out and leave the responsibility to host kernel. I prefer to prevent the system from booting if there is a failure in between. But as you mentioned in chat, if we need to keep parity with arm64 I am fine with letting the system boot. Allowing a system to boot will be the user accepting a compromised boot and whether or not we disable pkvm, the end state will be the same. we could probably have a tristate for kvm-intel.pkvm (disabled, enabled, permissive) where enable will prevent booting and permissive would boot in a compromised state :-). \n\nI fully agree with you regarding clearing the secrets if iommu init fails. I haven\u0027t tested reprivileging once init_finalize is successful as it does a lot more stuff like setting up EPT and such. It might be trivial and existing code may work, but need to test it. I shall test it and respond. If its not straight forward, I shall clear the pvmfw_rmem in case of iommu failure. v1 also did this, existing code doesn\u0027t do this as iommu init happened before pkvm takes over. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "e3e3b45d_52528b9a",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dd5a48a4_ccb5d82c",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-04T14:43:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I haven\u0027t tested reprivileging once init_finalize is successful as it does a lot more stuff like setting up EPT and such. It might be trivial and existing code may work, but need to test it. I shall test it and respond. If its not straight forward, I shall clear the pvmfw_rmem in case of iommu failure. v1 also did this, existing code doesn\u0027t do this as iommu init happened before pkvm takes over. What do you think?\n\nYep, that\u0027s more or less what I meant.\n\n\u003e we could probably have a tristate for kvm-intel.pkvm (disabled, enabled, permissive) where enable will prevent booting and permissive would boot in a compromised state :-).\n\nOr for example an extra flag to panic if pKVM init fails... We may implement something like that if we want to, though it doesn\u0027t seems to me that there is a need for that. As long as we take care to clear pvmfw secrets, the system is not exactly in a compromised state, it simply is not able to run protected VMs, that\u0027s it. (And if we are worried specifically about IOMMU driver failure and thus complete lack of DMA isolation, independently of pKVM, then such a forced panic should be a responsibility of the IOMMU driver, not pKVM, right?)",
      "parentUuid": "0ba32923_71fb8846",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2e466694_fdf4f2b6",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-10T15:01:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I have made the changes to clear pvmfw_rmem in case iommu_init fails. I have tried re-privileging but the boot doesn\u0027t go far after reprivilege because of various issues. I shall take a look at this later, for now lets clear the memory and proceed - what do you think?\n\n\u003e (And if we are worried specifically about IOMMU driver failure and thus complete lack of DMA isolation, independently of pKVM, then such a forced panic should be a responsibility of the IOMMU driver, not pKVM, right?)\n\nActually, I was not worried about IOMMU driver\u0027s failure causing security issue. I was mentioning about the DMA isolation that pkvm is not able to provide because of iommu driver failure. Without pkvm, I think its okay for boot to continue as IOMMU driver doesn\u0027t guarantee security in case of failure. But IMHO, pkvm should be able to provide the security it guarantees or should not allow the system to come up if it can\u0027t provide the guarantee. That was what I was trying to highlight.",
      "parentUuid": "dd5a48a4_ccb5d82c",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "75d0cff7_30eedb4a",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-12T00:13:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e But IMHO, pkvm should be able to provide the security it guarantees or should not allow the system to come up if it can\u0027t provide the guarantee.\n\nIt is still not clear to me why you think it should not allow the system to come up. As I said above: \"As long as we take care to clear pvmfw secrets, the system is not exactly in a compromised state, it simply is not able to run protected VMs, that\u0027s it.\" (And again, that is pKVM-ARM\u0027s approach as well.)",
      "parentUuid": "2e466694_fdf4f2b6",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3f157fa8_90ad712d",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T10:50:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My concern is we should not be giving the user a false sense of security. when the user enforces pkvm to be enabled, it means the user expected secrets to be protected, but also things like preventing host os from directly accessing protected devices, preventing compromised devices to access memory etc. The system coming up means the user is able to interact with the system which *could* be vulnerable to attacks. So, I was just proposing an idea where the system fails the boot early and let the user know. Or have a mechanism to notify the user explicitly that pkvm is disabled, and system is running in a degraded mode.\n\nI understand that the precedence is to let the system boot(as in pkvm arm). So I take back my arguments and rest my case :-)",
      "parentUuid": "75d0cff7_30eedb4a",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "03777b53_59c16423",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T12:48:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e but also things like preventing host os from directly accessing protected devices\n\nWe don\u0027t really support device assignment yet. And even if we did, it doesn\u0027t necessarily mean support for such use cases when the device needs to be protected from the host all the time, not just when it is assigned to a pVM. Although I can imagine such use cases, it is not clear whether we will ever need to support them. For instance, the existing pKVM-ARM\u0027s support for device assignment, if I\u0027m not mistaken, does not support such use cases, it assumes that a device only needs protection from the host throughout the lifecycle of a pVM it is assigned to, not the rest of the time.\n\n\u003e preventing compromised devices to access memory etc.\n\nAs we discussed, that is not pKVM\u0027s concern.\n\n\u003e Or have a mechanism to notify the user explicitly that pkvm is disabled\n\nThe lack of advertising KVM_X86_PKVM_PROTECTED_VM in kvm_caps.supported_vm_types is this mechanism, right?",
      "parentUuid": "3f157fa8_90ad712d",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ac267804_b84c5c17",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T13:12:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e\u003e preventing compromised devices to access memory etc.\n\n\u003e As we discussed, that is not pKVM\u0027s concern.\n\nAh ok, I thought this was indeed pkvms concern and thats one of the reasons we were pushing on disabling ATS.\n\n\u003e The lack of advertising KVM_X86_PKVM_PROTECTED_VM in kvm_caps.supported_vm_types is this mechanism, right?\n\nMy view of pkvm was just not its capability of creating and managing protected VM, but making sure that compromise of host os will not lead to compromised user data. This includes protecting secure devices from host access and making sure only devices get to access memory they are authorized to. If the goal of pkvm is only to make sure that protected vms will be *protected* from malicious access, and secure devices need to be protected only during the lifecycle of the VM, then I agree with you, letting the system boot with out pkvm on pkvm failure is perfectly fine",
      "parentUuid": "03777b53_59c16423",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "412b1885_d463a7e3",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T15:00:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Ah ok, I thought this was indeed pkvms concern and thats one of the reasons we were pushing on disabling ATS.\n\nIt is pKVM\u0027s concern if there are protected VMs running (or at least pKVM hypervisor itself is running, in particular it is holding pvmfw secrets in its memory), so pKVM needs to protect pVMs and itself from devices DMA.\n\n\u003e My view of pkvm was just not its capability of creating and managing protected VM, but making sure that compromise of host os will not lead to compromised user data.\n\npKVM can only ensure that for such user data that is protected from the host in the first place, i.e. for user data that is managed not by the host OS but by protected VMs.",
      "parentUuid": "ac267804_b84c5c17",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
